BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART;TZID=Asia/Chongqing:20261012T160000
DTEND;TZID=Asia/Chongqing:20261012T173000
LOCATION:S301\, NYU Shanghai New Bund Campus
SUMMARY:Aging in Diverse Populations: Social Contexts\, Health Outcomes\,
  and Life Experiences
DESCRIPTION:The Global Institute for Longevity and Resilience is hosting 
 a seminar titled "Aging in Diverse Populations: Social Contexts\, Health 
 Outcomes\, and Life Experiences."  Talk 1: Living Arrangements\, Loneline
 ss\, and Cognitive Function in Older Asian and Latin American Adults    S
 peaker: Qian Song\, Associate Professor &amp\; Undergraduate Program Dire
 ctor\, Donna M and Robert J Manning College of Nursing and Health Science
 s\, University of Massachusetts Boston      Focusing on social forces as 
 a fundamental cause of health inequities\, three major themes define Dr. 
 Song's scholarship. The first theme investigates migrants' lack of full c
 itizenship rights in their migration destinations as a cause of various h
 ealth disparities in late life. The second theme assesses the consequence
 s of family living arrangements and adult migration for the health and we
 ll-being of families considered "left-behind" in migrant sending communit
 ies. The third theme examines the short- and long-term effects of job lay
 offs on various health outcomes and health behaviors.&#160\;  Talk 2: The
  Many Faces of Aging in America: Immigrants\, Minorities\, and the Social
  Determinants of Health    Speaker: May Guo\, Professor\, School of Socia
 l Work\, University of Iowa      May (Man) Guo\, Ph.D.\, FGSA\, is a Prof
 essor in the School of Social Work at the University of Iowa. Dr. Guo's r
 esearch explores the intersection of immigration and aging\, addressing a
  broad range of topics\, including acculturation\, family relations\, soc
 ial support\, coping\, resilience\, and cognitive and mental health among
  older immigrants. By systematically investigating the social determinant
 s of health at the individual\, family\, neighborhood\, and societal leve
 ls among older immigrants\, Dr. Guo's work seeks to enhance the rigor of 
 aging theories\, which have traditionally been grounded in studies of Whi
 te\, non-migrant populations. Her recent research focuses on the nexus of
  race/ethnicity and immigration status\, exploring the diverse aging expe
 riences and health outcomes of minority older adults. An advocate for adv
 ancing gerontological social work\, Dr. Guo is dedicated to teaching\, me
 ntoring students\, and fostering cross-disciplinary collaborations to add
 ress the challenges faced by diverse aging populations.      Each talk wi
 ll be approximately 30 minutes. Followed by a Q&amp\;A session.  Introduc
 tion by: Bei Wu\, Provost and Distinguished Global Professor of Public He
 alth\, NYU Shanghai  This event is open to the NYU Shanghai Community. Yo
 u are encouraged to RSVP through here (https://engage.shanghai.nyu.edu/ev
 ent/12809882)\, but walk-ins are also welcome. Light refreshments will be
  provided.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<div class="DescriptionText"><p dir="ltr">  
   The Global Institute for Longevity and Resilience is hosting a seminar 
 titled "Aging in Diverse Populations: Social Contexts\, Health Outcomes\,
  and Life Experiences."  </p><p dir="ltr"><strong>Talk 1: Living Arrangem
 ents\, Loneliness\, and Cognitive Function in Older Asian and Latin Ameri
 can Adults</strong><br />    Speaker: Qian Song\, Associate Professor &am
 p\; Undergraduate Program Director\, Donna M and Robert J Manning College
  of Nursing and Health Sciences\, University of Massachusetts Boston  </p
 ><p>    Focusing on social forces as a fundamental cause of health inequi
 ties\, three major themes define Dr. Song's scholarship. The first theme 
 investigates migrants' lack of full citizenship rights in their migration
  destinations as a cause of various health disparities in late life. The 
 second theme assesses the consequences of family living arrangements and 
 adult migration for the health and well-being of families considered "lef
 t-behind" in migrant sending communities. The third theme examines the sh
 ort- and long-term effects of job layoffs on various health outcomes and 
 health behaviors.&#160\;  </p><p dir="ltr"><strong>Talk 2: The Many Faces
  of Aging in America: Immigrants\, Minorities\, and the Social Determinan
 ts of Health</strong><br />    Speaker: May Guo\, Professor\, School of S
 ocial Work\, University of Iowa  </p><p>    May (Man) Guo\, Ph.D.\, FGSA\
 , is a Professor in the School of Social Work at the University of Iowa. 
 Dr. Guo's research explores the intersection of immigration and aging\, a
 ddressing a broad range of topics\, including acculturation\, family rela
 tions\, social support\, coping\, resilience\, and cognitive and mental h
 ealth among older immigrants. By systematically investigating the social 
 determinants of health at the individual\, family\, neighborhood\, and so
 cietal levels among older immigrants\, Dr. Guo's work seeks to enhance th
 e rigor of aging theories\, which have traditionally been grounded in stu
 dies of White\, non-migrant populations. Her recent research focuses on t
 he nexus of race/ethnicity and immigration status\, exploring the diverse
  aging experiences and health outcomes of minority older adults. An advoc
 ate for advancing gerontological social work\, Dr. Guo is dedicated to te
 aching\, mentoring students\, and fostering cross-disciplinary collaborat
 ions to address the challenges faced by diverse aging populations.  </p><
 p dir="ltr">    Each talk will be approximately 30 minutes. Followed by a
  Q&amp\;A session.  </p><p dir="ltr"><strong>Introduction by:</strong> Be
 i Wu\, Provost and Distinguished Global Professor of Public Health\, NYU 
 Shanghai  </p><p dir="ltr"><strong>This event is open to the NYU Shanghai
  Community. You are encouraged to RSVP through <a href="https://engage.sh
 anghai.nyu.edu/event/12809882" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
 here</a>\, but walk-ins are also welcome. Light refreshments will be prov
 ided.</strong></p></div>
