BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261013T170000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261013T180000
LOCATION:7 East 12th Street\, New York\, New York\, 10003
GEO:40.734578;-73.993586
SUMMARY:Advancing Disaster Risk Reduction in a Changing World: Lessons Le
 arned from the Past in the U.S. and Japan—and Investments for the Futur
 e
DESCRIPTION:The need for competent disaster management continues to incre
 ase. In 2023\, the estimated economic loss of natural disasters worldwide
  was 380 billion USD\, with another 118 billion USD in insured losses. In
 creased need paired with scarce resources and inefficiencies in the curre
 nt response landscape will require improved solutions that are responsive
  to local needs and drive capacity-building.  The U.S. and Japan are unco
 ntested leaders in disaster risk reduction (DRR) and have similar risk pr
 ofiles so peer-to-peer learning would be valuable to decisionmakers in bo
 th countries. Despite having robust systems\, both the U.S. and Japan fac
 e persistent gaps in local-level capacity\, public-private coordination\,
  and the integration of innovation into resilience planning that affect t
 heir economic competitiveness and investment profile.  Two recent anniver
 saries—20 years since Hurricane Katrina (August 29\, 2025) and 15 years
  since the 3/11 Great East Japan Earthquake and tsunami disaster (March 1
 1\, 2026)\, the second and most costly disasters in economic losses respe
 ctively since 1900 —offered an opportunity to reflect on progress and c
 hart a forward-looking agenda. What have we learned since those watershed
  moments and what lessons can be gleaned for policymakers as they work to
  incentivize locally-led community action\, and especially private sector
 -led investment\, over the next 20 years? What do decisionmakers need to 
 prioritize to improve resilience and future-proof investments?  Chloe Dem
 rovsky will present the preliminary findings from her research project in
  partnership with Rui Kotani and Toshiya Hoshino from the University of O
 saka and generously supported by the US-Japan Foundation.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  The need for competent disaster managem
 ent continues to increase. In 2023\, the estimated economic loss of natur
 al disasters worldwide was 380 billion USD\, with another 118 billion USD
  in insured losses. Increased need paired with scarce resources and ineff
 iciencies in the current response landscape will require improved solutio
 ns that are responsive to local needs and drive capacity-building.</p><p>
   The U.S. and Japan are uncontested leaders in disaster risk reduction (
 DRR) and have similar risk profiles so peer-to-peer learning would be val
 uable to decisionmakers in both countries. Despite having robust systems\
 , both the U.S. and Japan face persistent gaps in local-level capacity\, 
 public-private coordination\, and the integration of innovation into resi
 lience planning that affect their economic competitiveness and investment
  profile.</p><p>  Two recent anniversaries—20 years since Hurricane Kat
 rina (August 29\, 2025) and 15 years since the 3/11 Great East Japan Eart
 hquake and tsunami disaster (March 11\, 2026)\, the second and most costl
 y disasters in economic losses respectively since 1900 —offered an oppo
 rtunity to reflect on progress and chart a forward-looking agenda. What h
 ave we learned since those watershed moments and what lessons can be glea
 ned for policymakers as they work to incentivize locally-led community ac
 tion\, and especially private sector-led investment\, over the next 20 ye
 ars? What do decisionmakers need to prioritize to improve resilience and 
 future-proof investments?</p><p>  Chloe Demrovsky will present the prelim
 inary findings from her research project in partnership with Rui Kotani a
 nd Toshiya Hoshino from the University of Osaka and generously supported 
 by the US-Japan Foundation.</p>
UID:20261013T210000Z-404861@events.nyu.edu
