LOCATION:Hemmerdinger Hall\, Washington Square East\, New York\, NY\, 100
 03\, United States
GEO:40.730409;-73.995732
SUMMARY:Chattel Like Cattle: Enslaved Women as Perfect Property
DESCRIPTION:CSAAD&#160\;2026-2027&#160\;Distinguished Scholar EventSir Hi
 lary Beckles\, Vice ChancellorThe University of the West IndiesTuesday\, 
 October 13\, 20265:30 pm – 7:00 pmNYU Silver Center\, Hemmerdinger Hall
 31 Washington PlNew York City\, NY 10003Zoom link:&#160\;https://nyu.zoom
 .us/j/93841506438The 'West' broke away from traditional forms of enslavem
 ent and invented an entirely new and unprecedented system that was chatte
 l slavery.&#160\;It was designed specifically for Africans who were legal
 ly classified as non-human. They were defined as property with all the fe
 atures of chattel.&#160\;They could be bought\, sold\, mortgaged\, bequea
 thed\, used as currency\, and were taxable. As non-humans they could be r
 aped\, prostituted\, and provided socio-sexual services.&#160\;African wo
 men were placed at the center of the Chattel system. It was through the b
 lack woman that the model became inter-generational with the market agree
 ment of her fertility and maternity. Her experience was therefore signifi
 cantly different from that of the African male. This lecture sets out how
  it was done across colonial Americas.Bio:The Vice Chancellor of The Univ
 ersity of the West Indies\, Sir Hilary Beckles is a distinguished academi
 c\, international thought leader\, United Nations committee official\, an
 d global public activist in the field of social justice and minority empo
 werment.Educated at the University of Hull\, Sir Hilary is a Director of 
 SAGICOR Financial Corporation\, the&#160\; largest financial company in t
 he Caribbean region\, Chairman of the Caribbean Examinations&#160\;Counci
 l\, former Chairman of The University of the West Indies Press\, Chairman
  of the Caribbean&#160\; Community (CARICOM)\, Commission on Reparation a
 nd Social Justice\, former President of Universities Caribbean\, an edito
 r of the UNESCO General History of Africa series and a Director&#160\; of
  the Global Tourism Resilience and Crisis Management Centre. He has also 
 served as an advisor&#160\; to the UN World Culture Report and Advisor to
  the Secretary General of the United Nations on&#160\; Sustainable Develo
 pment. He is a past Vice President of the International Task Force for th
 e UNESCO Slave Route Project and a past consultant for the UNESCO Cities 
 for Peace Global Programme. He sits on the Council of the Association of 
 Commonwealth Universities and is also&#160\;Chancellor of the United Nati
 ons University.Sir Beckles has Honorary Doctorate Awards from Kwame Nkrum
 ah University of Science and&#160\; Technology\, Hull University\, Brock 
 University\, University of Glasgow\, the University of&#160\; Johannesbur
 g\, North-West University\, and the University of London. Sir Hilary Beck
 les is also the&#160\; recipient of the prestigious Sir Martin Luther Kin
 g Jr Award for global advocacy\, academic&#160\; scholarship and intellec
 tual leadership in support of social justice\, institutional equity\, and
 &#160\; economic development for marginalized and oppressed ethnicities a
 nd nations.*This event is cosponsored by New York University&#160\;Center
  for Latin American and Caribbean Studies*For&#160\;accommodations\, plea
 se contact the Assistant Director for the Center for the Study of Africa 
 and the African Diaspora at ac8829@nyu.edu.Register Here&#160\;
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:CSAAD&#160\;2026-2027&#160\;Distinguished Sc
 holar Event<br />Sir Hilary Beckles\, Vice Chancellor<br />The University
  of the West Indies<br />Tuesday\, October 13\, 2026<br />5:30 pm – 7:0
 0 pm<br />NYU Silver Center\, Hemmerdinger Hall<br />31 Washington Pl<br 
 />New York City\, NY 10003<br />Zoom link:&#160\;https://nyu.zoom.us/j/93
 841506438<br />The 'West' broke away from traditional forms of enslavemen
 t and invented an entirely new and unprecedented system that was chattel 
 slavery.&#160\;It was designed specifically for Africans who were legally
  classified as non-human. They were defined as property with all the feat
 ures of chattel.&#160\;They could be bought\, sold\, mortgaged\, bequeath
 ed\, used as currency\, and were taxable. As non-humans they could be rap
 ed\, prostituted\, and provided socio-sexual services.&#160\;African wome
 n were placed at the center of the Chattel system. It was through the bla
 ck woman that the model became inter-generational with the market agreeme
 nt of her fertility and maternity. Her experience was therefore significa
 ntly different from that of the African male. This lecture sets out how i
 t was done across colonial Americas.<br />Bio:<br />The Vice Chancellor o
 f The University of the West Indies\, Sir Hilary Beckles is a distinguish
 ed academic\, international thought leader\, United Nations committee off
 icial\, and global public activist in the field of social justice and min
 ority empowerment.<br />Educated at the University of Hull\, Sir Hilary i
 s a Director of SAGICOR Financial Corporation\, the&#160\; largest financ
 ial company in the Caribbean region\, Chairman of the Caribbean Examinati
 ons&#160\;Council\, former Chairman of The University of the West Indies 
 Press\, Chairman of the Caribbean&#160\; Community (CARICOM)\, Commission
  on Reparation and Social Justice\, former President of Universities Cari
 bbean\, an editor of the UNESCO General History of Africa series and a Di
 rector&#160\; of the Global Tourism Resilience and Crisis Management Cent
 re. He has also served as an advisor&#160\; to the UN World Culture Repor
 t and Advisor to the Secretary General of the United Nations on&#160\; Su
 stainable Development. He is a past Vice President of the International T
 ask Force for the UNESCO Slave Route Project and a past consultant for th
 e UNESCO Cities for Peace Global Programme. He sits on the Council of the
  Association of Commonwealth Universities and is also&#160\;Chancellor of
  the United Nations University.<br />Sir Beckles has Honorary Doctorate A
 wards from Kwame Nkrumah University of Science and&#160\; Technology\, Hu
 ll University\, Brock University\, University of Glasgow\, the University
  of&#160\; Johannesburg\, North-West University\, and the University of L
 ondon. Sir Hilary Beckles is also the&#160\; recipient of the prestigious
  Sir Martin Luther King Jr Award for global advocacy\, academic&#160\; sc
 holarship and intellectual leadership in support of social justice\, inst
 itutional equity\, and&#160\; economic development for marginalized and o
 ppressed ethnicities and nations.<br />*This event is cosponsored by New 
 York University&#160\;Center for Latin American and Caribbean Studies*<br
  />For&#160\;accommodations\, please contact the Assistant Director for t
 he Center for the Study of Africa and the African Diaspora at ac8829@nyu.
 edu.<br />Register Here<br />&#160\;
