LOCATION:Bobst Library\, 7th Floor\, Rm. 745\, Bobst Library
SUMMARY:Mapping the Field (without AI) : Let's Get Critical: Arts & Human
 ities Research Day: How Do You Know?
DESCRIPTION:What is a scholarly field? How is it represented in catalogs 
 and other library resources? How can we stay up-to-date with new developm
 ents in a field? This workshop is intended to introduce students in the h
 umanities to different tools and strategies for mapping academic fields\,
  without the influence of AI algorithms which can reinforce existing bias
 es in publishing and citations. These strategies are scalable and can be 
 applied to literature reviews\, term papers\, qualifying exams and disser
 tations and theses.Stay after the workshop for free a pizza lunch provide
 d by the Division of Libraries! Dietary restrictions can be indicated in 
 the registration form.NYU Libraries is committed to providing reasonable 
 accommodations to individuals with disabilities. If you have an accommoda
 tion request\, please email lib-reference@nyu.edu. Additionally\, masks a
 re always welcome on NYU's campuses and facilities.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:What is a scholarly field? How is it represe
 nted in catalogs and other library resources? How can we stay up-to-date 
 with new developments in a field? This workshop is intended to introduce 
 students in the humanities to different tools and strategies for mapping 
 academic fields\, without the influence of AI algorithms which can reinfo
 rce existing biases in publishing and citations. These strategies are sca
 lable and can be applied to literature reviews\, term papers\, qualifying
  exams and dissertations and theses.<br />Stay after the workshop for fre
 e a pizza lunch provided by the Division of Libraries! Dietary restrictio
 ns can be indicated in the registration form.<br />NYU Libraries is commi
 tted to providing reasonable accommodations to individuals with disabilit
 ies. If you have an accommodation request\, please email lib-reference@ny
 u.edu. Additionally\, masks are always welcome on NYU's campuses and faci
 lities.
