DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261014T153000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261014T163000
LOCATION:Bobst Library\, 7th Floor\, Rm. 745\, Bobst Library
SUMMARY:Grad Student Lightning Talks: Let's Get Critical: Arts & Humaniti
 es Research Day: How Do You Know?
DESCRIPTION:Our rapidly evolving information ecosystem demands that all r
 esearchers—and\, perhaps\, especially grad students—review and adjust
  their research methods in response to questions such as: How do we find\
 , trace\, and use citations in the era of AI hallucinations? To what and 
 whom do we grant authority in a time marked by mis- and disinformation fr
 om figures and institutions previously regarded as relatively reliable? H
 ow might the particularities of the current publication landscape (rangin
 g from the use of published scholarly writing to train language learning 
 models to an increasing emphasis on open access) shape how and for whom w
 e write?The Let's Get Critical: Art and Humanities Research Day graduate 
 student lightning talks present Masters and Doctoral students in the arts
  and humanities at NYU with an opportunity to engage with these and simil
 ar questions in relation to their own work. This event also gives grad st
 udents an opportunity to actively contribute to interdisciplinary discuss
 ions about research methods in the current information landscape.The work
  of four current graduate students will be selected for presentation. Eac
 h speaker will be allotted ten minutes followed by a moderated panel disc
 ussion and Q&amp\;A.NYU Libraries is committed to providing reasonable ac
 commodations to individuals with disabilities. If you have an accommodati
 on request\, please email lib-reference@nyu.edu. Additionally\, masks are
  always welcome on NYU's campuses and facilities.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:Our rapidly evolving information ecosystem d
 emands that all researchers—and\, perhaps\, especially grad students—
 review and adjust their research methods in response to questions such as
 : How do we find\, trace\, and use citations in the era of AI hallucinati
 ons? To what and whom do we grant authority in a time marked by mis- and 
 disinformation from figures and institutions previously regarded as relat
 ively reliable? How might the particularities of the current publication 
 landscape (ranging from the use of published scholarly writing to train l
 anguage learning models to an increasing emphasis on open access) shape h
 ow and for whom we write?<br />The Let's Get Critical: Art and Humanities
  Research Day graduate student lightning talks present Masters and Doctor
 al students in the arts and humanities at NYU with an opportunity to enga
 ge with these and similar questions in relation to their own work. This e
 vent also gives grad students an opportunity to actively contribute to in
 terdisciplinary discussions about research methods in the current informa
 tion landscape.<br />The work of four current graduate students will be s
 elected for presentation. Each speaker will be allotted ten minutes follo
 wed by a moderated panel discussion and Q&amp\;A.<br />NYU Libraries is c
 ommitted to providing reasonable accommodations to individuals with disab
 ilities. If you have an accommodation request\, please email lib-referenc
 e@nyu.edu. Additionally\, masks are always welcome on NYU's campuses and 
 facilities.
