BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261014T173000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261014T183000
LOCATION:Institute for the Study of the Ancient World (ISAW)
GEO:40.78;-73.9603
SUMMARY:ISAW Exhibition Lecture: I Call Upon You\, Gods of the Night: Pro
 tecting against Witchcraft and Demons in Mesopotamia
DESCRIPTION:Registration is required. Please fill out the form at the bot
 tom of this page.  The ancient Mesopotamian world was populated with a wi
 de range of demonic and monstrous creatures\, all of which intersected wi
 th daily life in a variety of mostly-malignant ways. Most often\, demons\
 , or the chaotic figure of the witch\, could cause serious harm\, being a
 ssociated with disease and illness\, or even death\, particularly in infa
 nts and young children. But these forces could also be responsible for mo
 re mundane ills\, with even a run of bad luck being attributable to witch
 craft. This talk will discuss some of the major threats found in Mesopota
 mia—the demonic\, chaotic witch\; the demoness Lamashtu\, who was often
  responsible for infant mortality\; and others—examining in detail how 
 they could be safeguarded against\, or actively opposed once they were be
 lieved to be present. Mesopotamian ritual practices offered a range of po
 ssible options\, from the exhaustively (and expensively) detailed to simp
 ler\, even mass-produced\, remedies.  Gina Konstantopoulos (PhD\, Univers
 ity of Michigan\, 2015) is an Associate Professor in Assyriology and Cune
 iform Studies in the Department of Near Eastern Languages and Cultures at
  UCLA. Her research centers on religion\, magic\, and literature in Mesop
 otamia\, with a focus on the role of demons and monsters. Her monograph o
 n this topic\, The Divine/Demonic Seven and the Place of Demons in Mesopo
 tamia\, was published in 2023 by Brill. Her current research focuses on t
 wo major projects: one on the creation of distant and imagined lands and 
 notions of space and place in the ancient Near East\, and another on the 
 works of Enheduana and Sumerian myths of Inana.  This talk is given in co
 njunction with ISAW's exhibition Cursed! The Power of Magic in the Ancien
 t World. The exhibition is organized by the Toledo Museum of Art\; its pr
 esentation at ISAW is made possible by generous support from the Gladys K
 rieble Delmas Foundation and the Leon Levy Foundation.Please check isaw.n
 yu.edu (https://isaw.nyu.edu/) for event updates.ISAW is committed to pro
 viding a positive and educational experience for all guests and participa
 nts who attend our public programming. We ask that all attendees follow t
 he guidelines listed in our community standards policy (https://isaw.nyu.
 edu/events/community-standards-policy).
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p><em>Registration is required. Please fill
  out the form at the bottom of this page.</em></p><p>  The ancient Mesopo
 tamian world was populated with a wide range of demonic and monstrous cre
 atures\, all of which intersected with daily life in a variety of mostly-
 malignant ways. Most often\, demons\, or the chaotic figure of the witch\
 , could cause serious harm\, being associated with disease and illness\, 
 or even death\, particularly in infants and young children. But these for
 ces could also be responsible for more mundane ills\, with even a run of 
 bad luck being attributable to witchcraft. This talk will discuss some of
  the major threats found in Mesopotamia—the demonic\, chaotic witch\; t
 he demoness Lamashtu\, who was often responsible for infant mortality\; a
 nd others—examining in detail how they could be safeguarded against\, o
 r actively opposed once they were believed to be present. Mesopotamian ri
 tual practices offered a range of possible options\, from the exhaustivel
 y (and expensively) detailed to simpler\, even mass-produced\, remedies.<
 /p><p>  Gina Konstantopoulos (PhD\, University of Michigan\, 2015) is an 
 Associate Professor in Assyriology and Cuneiform Studies in the Departmen
 t of Near Eastern Languages and Cultures at UCLA. Her research centers on
  religion\, magic\, and literature in Mesopotamia\, with a focus on the r
 ole of demons and monsters. Her monograph on this topic\, <em>The Divine/
 Demonic Seven and the Place of Demons in Mesopotamia</em>\, was published
  in 2023 by Brill. Her current research focuses on two major projects: on
 e on the creation of distant and imagined lands and notions of space and 
 place in the ancient Near East\, and another on the works of Enheduana an
 d Sumerian myths of Inana.</p><p>  This talk is given in conjunction with
  ISAW's exhibition <em>Cursed! The Power of Magic in the Ancient World</e
 m>. The exhibition is organized by the Toledo Museum of Art\; its present
 ation at ISAW is made possible by generous support from the Gladys Kriebl
 e Delmas Foundation and the Leon Levy Foundation.</p><p><em>Please check 
 <a class="external-link" href="https://isaw.nyu.edu/" target="_self">isaw
 .nyu.edu</a> for event updates.</em></p><p><em>ISAW is committed to provi
 ding a positive and educational experience for all guests and participant
