DTSTART;TZID=Europe/Amsterdam:20261015T180000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/Amsterdam:20261015T190000
LOCATION:NYU Florence - Villa Sassetti
GEO:43.797299;11.262614
SUMMARY:Pontormo and the Florentine Tradition: Nostalgia during the Siege
  of Florence
DESCRIPTION:Studying the works of artists of previous generations was an 
 essential part of an artist's formation in Renaissance Florence. Jacopo P
 ontormo (1494-1557) was no exception to this rule and demonstrated unusua
 l sensitivity to the Florentine tradition throughout his career. However\
 , Pontormo created some of his most important works during the period of 
 the last Florentine republic and the dramatic siege that brought this sho
 rt-lived political experiment to an end (1527-1530). Both the preparation
 s of the Florentines for their defense and the ensuing siege brought sign
 ificant destruction to both public and private buildings and\, as a resul
 t\, to the artworks that they contained. Under these trying circumstances
 \, Pontormo appears to have paid even closer attention to Medieval and ea
 rlier Renaissance precedents. In this talk\, I will consider what occasio
 ned this increased attention to the art of the past and what its signific
 ance may have been for Pontormo and his contemporaries in Florence.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  Studying the works of artists of previo
 us generations was an essential part of an artist's formation in Renaissa
 nce Florence. Jacopo Pontormo (1494-1557) was no exception to this rule a
 nd demonstrated unusual sensitivity to the Florentine tradition throughou
 t his career. However\, Pontormo created some of his most important works
  during the period of the last Florentine republic and the dramatic siege
  that brought this short-lived political experiment to an end (1527-1530)
 . Both the preparations of the Florentines for their defense and the ensu
 ing siege brought significant destruction to both public and private buil
 dings and\, as a result\, to the artworks that they contained. Under thes
 e trying circumstances\, Pontormo appears to have paid even closer attent
 ion to Medieval and earlier Renaissance precedents. In this talk\, I will
  consider what occasioned this increased attention to the art of the past
  and what its significance may have been for Pontormo and his contemporar
 ies in Florence.</p>
UID:20261015T160000Z-403241@events.nyu.edu
