 tlement? And what might speculation come to mean as a practice of imagini
 ng otherwise\, rather than predicting return?<br /><br />  The title <em>
 Vitalisms</em> draws from Isabelle Graw's account of painting's "vitalist
 ic fantasies"—how vitality and agency have been projected onto painting
 . *<em>Vitalisms I</em> asked whether we could extend these fantasies and
  think of paintings as active participants in the historical and social r
 elations through which they are made\, encountered\, and circulated. <em>
 Vitalisms II</em> asks: What might happen when we approach a painting not
  simply as an image to be interpreted\, but as something with which we en
 ter into relation?<br /><br />  Like the first&#160\;<em>Vitalisms</em>\,
  this chapter is conceived as an experiment in which knowledge is exchang
 ed and produced throughout the exhibition. During weekly study sessions\,
  we spend extended time with the paintings\, developing interpretations c
 ollectively through close looking\, discussion\, and reflection. These en
 counters become part of the exhibition's research\, inviting us to ask: <
 strong>What might we come to value—and\, in turn\, make possible?</stro
