SUMMARY:CMEP Tending Hour
DESCRIPTION:To kick off our first Tending Hour session\, we will be cente
 ring creativity\, writing\, and cultural communal joy traditions.  As we 
 build\, we will write affirmations and inspirations directly onto our phy
 sical crafts\, turning each object into a personal totem of reflection. W
 e invite students to honor diverse traditions and practices as we allow o
 ur bodies to slow down. We will design the following:Piñata: A tradition
 al Mexican and Latin American container filled with candy that is hung up
  and broken during times of celebration. We will be mirroring this tradit
 ion to create a moment of shared communal joy and physical release.  Pape
 r Lantern: A traditional East and Southeast Asian decoration used across 
 many cultures. We will be making Chinese paper lanterns\, which represent
  prosperity\, luck\, and health.    &#160\;  Snacks will be provided.  Th
 is event is free and open to all NYU students\, inclusive of all backgrou
 nds and identities.  &#160\;Register to Attend Event (https://forms.gle/7
 vbnTjpfi1reXp1b8)  &#160\;**Note on Inclusion and Accessibility: Our even
 ts and initiatives are open to all backgrounds and identities. It is a pr
 iority to make our events inclusive and accessible. For any questions or 
 to notify us of a request\, please email cmep@nyu.edu at least 72 hours b
 efore the event.**
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  To kick off our first Tending Hour sess
 ion\, we will be centering creativity\, writing\, and cultural communal j
 oy traditions.</p><p>  As we build\, we will write affirmations and inspi
 rations directly onto our physical crafts\, turning each object into a pe
 rsonal totem of reflection. We invite students to honor diverse tradition
 s and practices as we allow our bodies to slow down. We will design the f
 ollowing:</p><ul><li><strong>Piñata:</strong> A traditional Mexican and 
 Latin American container filled with candy that is hung up and broken dur
 ing times of celebration. We will be mirroring this tradition to create a
  moment of shared communal joy and physical release.  </li><li><strong>Pa
 per Lantern:</strong> A traditional East and Southeast Asian decoration u
 sed across many cultures. We will be making Chinese paper lanterns\, whic
 h represent prosperity\, luck\, and health.  </li></ul><p>  &#160\;</p><p
 >  Snacks will be provided.</p><p>  This event is free and open to all NY
 U students\, inclusive of all backgrounds and identities.</p><p>  &#160\;
