DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261020T170000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261020T183000
LOCATION:20 Cooper Square\, New York\, NY
SUMMARY:Polio Vaccines: Wanted Dead or Alive
DESCRIPTION:Hosted by NYU Center for the HumanitiesA conversation featuri
 ng David Oshinsky\, Frances Bernstein\, Perri Klass\, and Trace Jordan&#1
 60\;The development of polio vaccines in the 1950s gave the ability to pr
 otect children against a crippling and often fatal disease that had cast 
 an ominous shadow for decades. But medical science had simultaneously gen
 erated two types of polio vaccine – one that contained killed polio vir
 us\, developed and championed by Dr. Jonas Salk\, and one that included l
 ive but attenuated polio virus\, discovered by his bitter rival and publi
 c critic\, Dr. Albert Sabin. Prompted by a new biography of Sabin\, this 
 panel presentation explores the contrasting personalities of the two scie
 ntists\, how the vaccines were originally tested (an unexpected excursion
  into the Soviet Union during the height of the Cold War)\, and the risks
  and benefits of each vaccine type. Debates and decisions about the optim
 al polio vaccine continue today\, shaping the daily practice of pediatric
 ians and public health workers throughout the United States and around th
 e world.&#160\;The event will feature NYU Professors David Oshinsky (Prof
 essor of History and Director of the Division of Medical Humanities at NY
 U Grossman School of Medicine)\, Frances Bernstein (Clinical Professor\, 
 Russian &amp\; Slavic Studies)\, and Perri Klass (Professor of Journalism
  and Pediatrics). The panel moderator is Trace Jordan (Clinical Professor
 \, College Core Curriculum).&#160\;Brought to you by the Medical Humaniti
 es Working Group.https://publichealth.nyu.edu/events-news/events/2026/10/
 20/polio-vaccines-wanted-dead-or-alive (https://publichealth.nyu.edu/even
 ts-news/events/2026/10/20/polio-vaccines-wanted-dead-or-alive)
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:Hosted by NYU Center for the HumanitiesA con
 versation featuring David Oshinsky\, Frances Bernstein\, Perri Klass\, an
 d Trace Jordan&#160\;The development of polio vaccines in the 1950s gave 
 the ability to protect children against a crippling and often fatal disea
 se that had cast an ominous shadow for decades. But medical science had s
 imultaneously generated two types of polio vaccine – one that contained
  killed polio virus\, developed and championed by Dr. Jonas Salk\, and on
 e that included live but attenuated polio virus\, discovered by his bitte
 r rival and public critic\, Dr. Albert Sabin. Prompted by a new biography
  of Sabin\, this panel presentation explores the contrasting personalitie
 s of the two scientists\, how the vaccines were originally tested (an une
 xpected excursion into the Soviet Union during the height of the Cold War
 )\, and the risks and benefits of each vaccine type. Debates and decision
 s about the optimal polio vaccine continue today\, shaping the daily prac
 tice of pediatricians and public health workers throughout the United Sta
 tes and around the world.&#160\;The event will feature NYU Professors Dav
 id Oshinsky (Professor of History and Director of the Division of Medical
  Humanities at NYU Grossman School of Medicine)\, Frances Bernstein (Clin
 ical Professor\, Russian &amp\; Slavic Studies)\, and Perri Klass (Profes
 sor of Journalism and Pediatrics). The panel moderator is Trace Jordan (C
 linical Professor\, College Core Curriculum).&#160\;Brought to you by the
  Medical Humanities Working Group.<br /><a href="https://publichealth.nyu
 .edu/events-news/events/2026/10/20/polio-vaccines-wanted-dead-or-alive" t
 arget="_blank">https://publichealth.nyu.edu/events-news/events/2026/10/20
 /polio-vaccines-wanted-dead-or-alive</a>
