DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261020T190000
LOCATION:Institute for the Study of the Ancient World (ISAW)
GEO:40.78;-73.9603
SUMMARY:ISAW Expanding the Ancient World Workshop: "From Silver Mines to 
 the Palatine: Slavery in the Roman World"
DESCRIPTION:Registration is required. Please fill out the form at the bot
 tom of this page.  &#160\;Expanding the Ancient World (https://isaw.nyu.e
 du/outreach) is a series of professional development workshops and online
  resources for teachers. Keyed to the NYC Department of Education Social 
 Studies Scope and Sequence\, this program is designed to offer K-12 educa
 tors opportunities to develop their knowledge of the ancient world and to
  provide classroom-ready strategies for teaching the past with reliable s
 ources. Featuring inquiry-based workshops\, flexible lesson plans\, and u
 p-to-date research\, Expanding the Ancient World aims to equip teachers w
 ith information and skills that they can share with their students. CTLE 
 credits will be offered to New York State teachers.  The workshop invites
  teachers to further incorporate Roman slavery into ancient history curri
 cula. From transactional papyri\, to clever slaves on stage\, to the tomb
 stone of a child miner\, we will examine slavery both as a ubiquitous leg
 al phenomenon and as a varied spectrum of experience across the Roman wor
 ld. That is\, we will engage with not just the testimonies of the literar
 y elite—largely that of enslavers—but also traces of enslaved people'
 s lives in material and visual culture. Grounded in a series of concrete 
 case studies\, we will discuss how to present primary sources that will r
 equire students to confront some of the most challenging\, and fascinatin
 g\, aspects of ancient history.  We will focus on several questions: What
  was slavery in the Roman Empire? Who was enslaved\, and how did they bec
 ome enslaved? How can we learn about them? What can studying Roman slaver
 y contribute to our understanding of both slavery and Roman history? We w
 ill also analyze together how the ancient phenomenon differed from more r
 ecent slavery\, such as the Transatlantic slave trade\, because of the na
 ture of different practices\, evidence preservation\, and conceptions of 
 race and ethnicity. However\, the legacy of ancient slavery had a profoun
 d impact on enslavers' worldviews in the Antebellum South and beyond\; we
  will untangle historians' anachronistic assumptions to effectively\, and
  critically\, think across periods. Ultimately\, the aim is to present st
 udents with an even deeper and more accurate understanding of everyday li
 fe and human experience in the ancient world.  Workshop led by Charlotte 
 Mandy (https://classicalstudies.columbia.edu/students/charlotte-mandy) (P
 hD Student\, Columbia University Classical Studies).  Participants will r
 eceive 1.5 CTLE hours.If you have any questions regarding the Expanding t
 he Ancient World program please email ETAW@nyu.edu (mailto:ETAW@nyu.edu).
 Please check isaw.nyu.edu (http://isaw.nyu.edu/) for event updates.ISAW i
 s committed to providing a positive and educational experience for all gu
 ests and participants who attend our public programming. We ask that all 
 attendees follow the guidelines listed in our community standards policy 
 (http://isaw.nyu.edu/events/community-standards-policy).
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<h5><strong><em>Registration is required. Pl
 ease fill out the form at the bottom of this page.<a class="external-link
 " href="https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSdnphF1cS7-JSnFxoe7HAAel
 GJ4ufS2P1l8CEY8LC1NOgQQ4A/viewform?usp=publish-editor" target="_self"><br
  /></a></em></strong></h5><p>  &#160\;</p><p><em><a href="https://isaw.ny
 u.edu/outreach" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Expanding the Ancient Worl
 d</a></em> is a series of professional development workshops and online r
 esources for teachers. Keyed to the NYC Department of Education Social St
 udies Scope and Sequence\, this program is designed to offer K-12 educato
 rs opportunities to develop their knowledge of the ancient world and to p
 rovide classroom-ready strategies for teaching the past with reliable sou
 rces. Featuring inquiry-based workshops\, flexible lesson plans\, and up-
 to-date research\, Expanding the Ancient World aims to equip teachers wit
 h information and skills that they can share with their students. CTLE cr
 edits will be offered to New York State teachers.</p><p>  The workshop in
 vites teachers to further incorporate Roman slavery into ancient history 
 curricula. From transactional papyri\, to clever slaves on stage\, to the
  tombstone of a child miner\, we will examine slavery both as a ubiquitou
 s legal phenomenon and as a varied spectrum of experience across the Roma
 n world. That is\, we will engage with not just the testimonies of the li
 terary elite—largely that of enslavers—but also traces of enslaved pe
 ople's lives in material and visual culture. Grounded in a series of conc
 rete case studies\, we will discuss how to present primary sources that w
 ill require students to confront some of the most challenging\, and fasci
 nating\, aspects of ancient history.</p><p>  We will focus on several que
 stions: What was slavery in the Roman Empire? Who was enslaved\, and how 
 did they become enslaved? How can we learn about them? What can studying 
 Roman slavery contribute to our understanding of both slavery and Roman h
 istory? We will also analyze together how the ancient phenomenon differed
  from more recent slavery\, such as the Transatlantic slave trade\, becau
 se of the nature of different practices\, evidence preservation\, and con
 ceptions of race and ethnicity. However\, the legacy of ancient slavery h
 ad a profound impact on enslavers' worldviews in the Antebellum South and
  beyond\; we will untangle historians' anachronistic assumptions to effec
 tively\, and critically\, think across periods. Ultimately\, the aim is t
 o present students with an even deeper and more accurate understanding of
  everyday life and human experience in the ancient world.</p><p>  Worksho
 p led by <a class="external-link" href="https://classicalstudies.columbia
 .edu/students/charlotte-mandy" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Charlotte M
 andy</a> (PhD Student\, Columbia University Classical Studies).</p><p>  P
 articipants will receive 1.5 CTLE hours.</p><p><em>If you have any questi
 ons regarding the Expanding the Ancient World program please email <a cla
 ss="email-link" href="mailto:ETAW@nyu.edu" target="_self">ETAW@nyu.edu</a
 >.</em></p><p><em>Please check <a class="external-link" href="http://isaw
 .nyu.edu/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">isaw.nyu.edu</a> for event upda
 tes.</em></p><p><em>ISAW is committed to providing a positive and educati
 onal experience for all guests and participants who attend our public pro
 gramming. We ask that all attendees follow the guidelines listed in our <
 a class="external-link" href="http://isaw.nyu.edu/events/community-standa
 rds-policy" target="_blank" rel="noopener">community standards policy</a>
 .</em></p>
