SUMMARY:Where is the Care?
DESCRIPTION:Dejha Carrington is an arts leader. In 2017\, she co-founded 
 Commissioner\, a collaborative model and social practice that brings loca
 ls together to support contemporary artists and collect art in their citi
 es. As Executive Director\, she has cultivated a community of over 850 me
 mbers to date\, collectively funding more than 50 curated artist commissi
 ons in Miami\, Detroit\, Montréal\, and Mexico City. From 2015 to 2022\,
  she served as Vice President of Strategic Communications for YoungArts. 
 She is CBVC's 2026–27 Distinguished Scholar-in-Residence and teaches th
 e Business of Art at New World School of the Arts at Miami-Dade College\,
  dividing her time between Miami\, Montréal\, and New York.Damien Davis 
 is a Brooklyn-based artist and curator\, born in Crowley\, Louisiana and 
 raised in Phoenix\, Arizona. His practice explores historical representat
 ions of Blackness\, unpacking the visual language of different cultures a
 nd questioning how those societies code and decode representations of rac
 e through craft\, design\, and digital modes of production. Using digital
  tools to shape layers of natural and synthetic materials\, Davis compose
 s stories through a lexicon of graphic figures and shapes that move betwe
 en personal memory and chapters of human history\, using color as both ae
 sthetic and metaphor to create space for confronting bias and dehumanizat
 ion in contemporary society.  His work has appeared at the Metropolitan M
 useum of Art\, the Museum of Arts and Design\, The Whitney Museum\, and t
 he Museum of Modern Art\, as well as METHOD Gallery in Seattle and Biagio
 tti Progetto Arte in Italy. He holds a Master's degree in Visual Arts Adm
 inistration from NYU and is the recipient of the Rema Hort Mann Foundatio
 n Community Engagement Grant\, with residencies at the Museum of Arts and
  Design\, Lower Manhattan Cultural Council\, and Pilchuck Glass School. D
 avis is a former fellow and current advisor for the Art &amp\; Law Progra
 m in New York City. His work has been featured in The New York Times\, Fr
 ieze Magazine\, The Guardian\, Hyperallergic\, and Vulture.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p><strong>Dejha Carrington</strong> is an a
 rts leader. In 2017\, she co-founded Commissioner\, a collaborative model
  and social practice that brings locals together to support contemporary 
 artists and collect art in their cities. As Executive Director\, she has 
 cultivated a community of over 850 members to date\, collectively funding
  more than 50 curated artist commissions in Miami\, Detroit\, Montréal\,
  and Mexico City. From 2015 to 2022\, she served as Vice President of Str
 ategic Communications for YoungArts. She is CBVC's 2026–27 Distinguishe
 d Scholar-in-Residence and teaches the Business of Art at New World Schoo
 l of the Arts at Miami-Dade College\, dividing her time between Miami\, M
 ontréal\, and New York.</p><p><strong>Damien Davis</strong> is a Brookly
 n-based artist and curator\, born in Crowley\, Louisiana and raised in Ph
 oenix\, Arizona. His practice explores historical representations of Blac
 kness\, unpacking the visual language of different cultures and questioni
 ng how those societies code and decode representations of race through cr
 aft\, design\, and digital modes of production. Using digital tools to sh
 ape layers of natural and synthetic materials\, Davis composes stories th
 rough a lexicon of graphic figures and shapes that move between personal 
 memory and chapters of human history\, using color as both aesthetic and 
 metaphor to create space for confronting bias and dehumanization in conte
 mporary society.</p><p>  His work has appeared at the Metropolitan Museum
  of Art\, the Museum of Arts and Design\, The Whitney Museum\, and the Mu
 seum of Modern Art\, as well as METHOD Gallery in Seattle and Biagiotti P
 rogetto Arte in Italy. He holds a Master's degree in Visual Arts Administ
 ration from NYU and is the recipient of the Rema Hort Mann Foundation Com
 munity Engagement Grant\, with residencies at the Museum of Arts and Desi
 gn\, Lower Manhattan Cultural Council\, and Pilchuck Glass School. Davis 
 is a former fellow and current advisor for the Art &amp\; Law Program in 
 New York City. His work has been featured in <i>The New York Times</i>\, 
 <i>Frieze Magazine</i>\, <i>The Guardian</i>\, <i>Hyperallergic</i>\, and
  <i>Vulture</i>.</p>
