DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261021T173000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261021T190000
LOCATION:Main Event Space\, Rm. 101\, 105 East 17th Street
GEO:40.736399;-73.988013
SUMMARY:The Rise of Dominican American Political Power in the United States
DESCRIPTION:In Politics in Our Veins\, Yalidy Matos\, Domingo Morel\, and
  Michelle Bueno Vásquez chart the rise of Dominican American political p
 ower across the United States\, exploring the myriad factors that have co
 ntributed to their political success as thoughtful citizens\, activists\,
  and elected officials. On October 21\, join us for a panel conversation 
 marking the publication of the new book with the authors alongside Ramona
  Hernandez\, Raquel Batista\, and Cristina Beltrán. This event takes pla
 ce at NYU Wagner (105 East 17th Street). Doors will open at 5 pm\, and th
 e event begins at 5:30 pm.About the BookDominican Americans are one of th
 e largest and fastest-growing Latinx groups in the United States\, with a
  population that has quadrupled from 517\,000 to a little over 2.3 millio
 n as of 2023. While New York City is home to the largest Dominican commun
 ity in the country—and is where most Dominican American elected officia
 ls (DEOs) are from—Dominican Americans continue to increase their repre
 sentation nationwide. Learn more via NYU Press.PanelistsRaquel Batista is
  an experienced civil rights and social impact leader in government\, cor
 porate and non-profit spaces. She previously served as the Commissioner o
 f the NYC Mayor's Office of Immigrant Affairs\, as Executive Director of 
 the Northern Manhattan Coalition for Immigrant Rights and more recently a
 t the New York City Asylum Application Center assisting hundreds of recen
 tly arrived migrants with their humanitarian applications. A second-gener
 ation Afro-Dominicana\, Latina\, and lifelong New Yorker\, Ms. Batista ha
 s been advocating for the rights of immigrant New Yorkers for over two de
 cades.Ramona Hernández is a Distinguished Professor of Sociology and the
  Director of the CUNY Dominican Studies Institute\, both at The City Coll
 ege of New York. She also holds faculty affiliations with the Internation
 al Migration MA Program and the Sociology PhD Program at the CUNY Graduat
 e Center. She is also the Executive Director of the Inter-University Prog
 ram for Latino Research. Dr. Hernández's latest publication\, From Immig
 rants to Settled People: Dominicans in the United States\, co-authored wi
 th Gregory Morton\, is forthcoming from Lived Places Publishing Press. Sh
 e was also recently selected as one of the 2026 Latino Trailblazers by Ci
 ty &amp\; State New York.Yalidy Matos is Associate Professor of Political
  Science at Rutgers University. She is the author of Moral and Immoral Wh
 iteness in Immigration Politics. Matos has been the recipient of the 2022
  American Political Science Association Distinguished Junior Scholar in P
 olitical Psychology Award and a 2024-2025 Russel Sage Foundation Visiting
  Scholar.Domingo Morel is Associate Professor of Political Science and Pu
 blic Service at the Robert F. Wagner School of Public Service at New York
  University. He is the author and co-editor of several books\, including 
 Takeover: Race\, Education\, and American Democracy\, which won the W.E.B
 . Du Bois Distinguished Book Award\, and most recently Developing Scholar
 s: Race\, Politics\, and the Pursuit of Higher Education.Michelle Bueno V
 ásquez is a Doctoral Student in Political Science at Northwestern Univer
 sity.Cristina Beltrán works at the intersection of Latinx politics and p
 olitical theory. She is an associate professor and director of graduate s
 tudies in the Department of Social and Cultural Analysis. Her work has ap
 peared in Political Theory\, the Du Bois Review\, Aztlán: A Journal of C
 hicano Studies\, Political Research Quarterly\, and various edited volume
 s. She is currently the co-editor of Theory &amp\; Event\, a peer-reviewe
 d journal that publishes work by scholars working at the intersections of
  political theory\, cultural theory\, political economy\, aesthetics\, ph
 ilosophy\, and the arts. She is also an occasional guest on MSNBC.There w
 ill be a small reception to follow afterward.RSVP: https://wagner.nyu.edu
 /community/events/rise-dominican-american-political-power-united-states-2
 026-10-21 (https://wagner.nyu.edu/community/events/rise-dominican-america
 n-political-power-united-states-2026-10-21)
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:In Politics in Our Veins\, <strong style="bo
 x-sizing:border-box\;font-synthesis:none\;">Yalidy Matos</strong>\, <stro
 ng style="box-sizing:border-box\;font-synthesis:none\;">Domingo Morel</st
 rong>\, and <strong style="box-sizing:border-box\;font-synthesis:none\;">
 Michelle Bueno Vásquez</strong> chart the rise of Dominican American pol
 itical power across the United States\, exploring the myriad factors that
  have contributed to their political success as thoughtful citizens\, act
 ivists\, and elected officials. On October 21\, join us for a panel conve
 rsation marking the publication of the new book with the authors alongsid
 e <strong style="box-sizing:border-box\;font-synthesis:none\;">Ramona Her
 nandez</strong>\, <strong style="box-sizing:border-box\;font-synthesis:no
 ne\;">Raquel Batista</strong>\, and <strong style="box-sizing:border-box\
 ;font-synthesis:none\;">Cristina Beltrán</strong>. This event takes plac
 e at NYU Wagner (105 East 17th Street). Doors will open at 5 pm\, and the
  event begins at 5:30 pm.<br /><strong style="box-sizing:border-box\;font
 -synthesis:none\;">About the Book</strong><br />Dominican Americans are o
 ne of the largest and fastest-growing Latinx groups in the United States\
 , with a population that has quadrupled from 517\,000 to a little over 2.
 3 million as of 2023. While New York City is home to the largest Dominica
 n community in the country—and is where most Dominican American elected
  officials (DEOs) are from—Dominican Americans continue to increase the
 ir representation nationwide. Learn more via NYU Press.<br /><strong styl
 e="box-sizing:border-box\;font-synthesis:none\;">Panelists</strong><br />
 <strong style="box-sizing:border-box\;font-synthesis:none\;">Raquel Batis
 ta</strong> is an experienced civil rights and social impact leader in go
 vernment\, corporate and non-profit spaces. She previously served as the 
 Commissioner of the NYC Mayor's Office of Immigrant Affairs\, as Executiv
 e Director of the Northern Manhattan Coalition for Immigrant Rights and m
 ore recently at the New York City Asylum Application Center assisting hun
 dreds of recently arrived migrants with their humanitarian applications. 
 A second-generation Afro-Dominicana\, Latina\, and lifelong New Yorker\, 
 Ms. Batista has been advocating for the rights of immigrant New Yorkers f
 or over two decades.<br /><strong style="box-sizing:border-box\;font-synt
 hesis:none\;">Ramona Hernández</strong> is a Distinguished Professor of 
 Sociology and the Director of the CUNY Dominican Studies Institute\, both
  at The City College of New York. She also holds faculty affiliations wit
 h the International Migration MA Program and the Sociology PhD Program at
  the CUNY Graduate Center. She is also the Executive Director of the Inte
 r-University Program for Latino Research. Dr. Hernández's latest publica
 tion\, From Immigrants to Settled People: Dominicans in the United States
 \, co-authored with Gregory Morton\, is forthcoming from Lived Places Pub
 lishing Press. She was also recently selected as one of the 2026 Latino T
 railblazers by City &amp\; State New York.<br /><strong style="box-sizing
 :border-box\;font-synthesis:none\;">Yalidy Matos</strong> is Associate Pr
 ofessor of Political Science at Rutgers University. She is the author of 
 Moral and Immoral Whiteness in Immigration Politics. Matos has been the r
 ecipient of the 2022 American Political Science Association Distinguished
  Junior Scholar in Political Psychology Award and a 2024-2025 Russel Sage
  Foundation Visiting Scholar.<br /><strong style="box-sizing:border-box\;
 font-synthesis:none\;">Domingo Morel</strong> is Associate Professor of P
 olitical Science and Public Service at the Robert F. Wagner School of Pub
 lic Service at New York University. He is the author and co-editor of sev
 eral books\, including Takeover: Race\, Education\, and American Democrac
 y\, which won the W.E.B. Du Bois Distinguished Book Award\, and most rece
 ntly Developing Scholars: Race\, Politics\, and the Pursuit of Higher Edu
 cation.<br /><strong style="box-sizing:border-box\;font-synthesis:none\;"
 >Michelle Bueno Vásquez</strong> is a Doctoral Student in Political Scie
 nce at Northwestern University.<br /><strong style="box-sizing:border-box
 \;font-synthesis:none\;">Cristina Beltrán</strong> works at the intersec
 tion of Latinx politics and political theory. She is an associate profess
 or and director of graduate studies in the Department of Social and Cultu
 ral Analysis. Her work has appeared in Political Theory\, the Du Bois Rev
 iew\, Aztlán: A Journal of Chicano Studies\, Political Research Quarterl
 y\, and various edited volumes. She is currently the co-editor of Theory 
 &amp\; Event\, a peer-reviewed journal that publishes work by scholars wo
 rking at the intersections of political theory\, cultural theory\, politi
 cal economy\, aesthetics\, philosophy\, and the arts. She is also an occa
 sional guest on MSNBC.<br />There will be a small reception to follow aft
 erward.<br />RSVP: <a href="https://wagner.nyu.edu/community/events/rise-
 dominican-american-political-power-united-states-2026-10-21" target="_bla
 nk">https://wagner.nyu.edu/community/events/rise-dominican-american-polit
 ical-power-united-states-2026-10-21</a>
UID:20261021T213000Z-406902@events.nyu.edu
