DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261021T173000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261021T193000
LOCATION:Institute for the Study of the Ancient World (ISAW)
GEO:40.78;-73.9603
SUMMARY:ISAW Lecture: Burial and Betrayal Punishing Social Transgressions
  in the Ancient Roman Cemetery
DESCRIPTION:Registration is required. Please fill out the form at the bot
 tom of this page.Abstract  The ancient Roman cemetery is often framed as 
 the domain of the "well deserving\," as funerary monuments frequently for
 mulaically assert. These dead were deserving of proper burial\, of commem
 oration\, and of the ongoing provision of offerings and other cult activi
 ties that both integrated them into the community of the dead and maintai
 ned their relationships with the living. But what might happen to the "ba
 dly deserving?" By examining select case studies of social betrayal docum
 ented in Roman cemeteries from the first three centuries CE\, this talk w
 ill explore efforts at enforcing postmortem punishments against those per
 ceived to have violated Roman social norms. It will argue that these stor
 ies of betrayal\, while unique in their specifics\, together illuminate t
 he ways in which private individuals could seek to impose a form of after
 life exile upon those they deemed guilty of social transgressions.About t
 he speaker  Darcy Tuttle is a Research Scholar at ISAW. Her research draw
 s on historical\, archaeological\, epigraphic\, and literary evidence to 
 explore how people engaged with and related to the dead in the ancient It
 aly. She is an active field archaeologist with involvement in two project
 s based in Italy: the Gabii Project and the Southern Etruscan Tomb Survey
 . She recently completed her PhD at the University of California\, Berkel
 ey in the Graduate Group in Ancient History and Mediterranean Archaeology
 . Prior to joining ISAW\, she was the recipient of the 2026 Donald and Ma
 ria Cox | Dorothy and Lewis B. Cullman Rome Prize at the American Academy
  in Rome. She is currently working on a book project provisionally entitl
 ed\, "Ghost Justice: Crime\, Community\, and the Roman Cult of the Dead."
 Please check isaw.nyu.edu (http://isaw.nyu.edu/) for event updates.ISAW i
 s committed to providing a positive and educational experience for all gu
 ests and participants who attend our public programming. We ask that all 
 attendees follow the guidelines listed in our community standards policy 
 (https://isaw.nyu.edu/events/community-standards-policy).
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p><em>Registration is required. Please fill
  out the form at the bottom of this page.</em></p><p><strong>Abstract</st
 rong><br />  The ancient Roman cemetery is often framed as the domain of 
 the "well deserving\," as funerary monuments frequently formulaically ass
 ert. These dead were deserving of proper burial\, of commemoration\, and 
 of the ongoing provision of offerings and other cult activities that both
  integrated them into the community of the dead and maintained their rela
 tionships with the living. But what might happen to the "badly deserving?
 " By examining select case studies of social betrayal documented in Roman
  cemeteries from the first three centuries CE\, this talk will explore ef
 forts at enforcing postmortem punishments against those perceived to have
  violated Roman social norms. It will argue that these stories of betraya
 l\, while unique in their specifics\, together illuminate the ways in whi
 ch private individuals could seek to impose a form of afterlife exile upo
 n those they deemed guilty of social transgressions.</p><p><strong>About 
 the speaker</strong><br />  Darcy Tuttle is a Research Scholar at ISAW. H
 er research draws on historical\, archaeological\, epigraphic\, and liter
 ary evidence to explore how people engaged with and related to the dead i
 n the ancient Italy. She is an active field archaeologist with involvemen
 t in two projects based in Italy: the Gabii Project and the Southern Etru
 scan Tomb Survey. She recently completed her PhD at the University of Cal
 ifornia\, Berkeley in the Graduate Group in Ancient History and Mediterra
 nean Archaeology. Prior to joining ISAW\, she was the recipient of the 20
 26 Donald and Maria Cox | Dorothy and Lewis B. Cullman Rome Prize at the 
 American Academy in Rome. She is currently working on a book project prov
 isionally entitled\, "Ghost Justice: Crime\, Community\, and the Roman Cu
 lt of the Dead."</p><p><em>Please check <a class="external-link" href="ht
 tp://isaw.nyu.edu/" target="_self">isaw.nyu.edu</a> for event updates.</e
 m><br /><br /><em>ISAW is committed to providing a positive and education
 al experience for all guests and participants who attend our public progr
 amming. We ask that all attendees follow the guidelines listed in our <a 
 href="https://isaw.nyu.edu/events/community-standards-policy" target="_bl
 ank" rel="noopener">community standards policy</a>.</em></p>
