SUMMARY:Saving St. Andrew's Chapel: Traversing the Heritage / Sustainabil
 ity Pendulum in the UK Today
DESCRIPTION:The presentation will explore the many\, often conflicting he
 ritage and sustainability challenges in bringing a derelict mediaeval cha
 pel back into use as a contemporary home in rural England. British archit
 ect Malcolm Fryer will outline his work\, focusing on the ongoing project
  to repair and improve the St. Andrew's Chapel at Boxley in Kent. He will
  share details about his work with Historic England on thermal\, moisture
  and airtightness performance as a case study that suggests directions an
 d practices for the integration of efficiency and modernization into hist
 oric structures. Professor Fryer will also share his experience in taking
  NYU MA students to visit the project over the last few years\, highlight
 ing opportunities to participate in projects\, investigating and contribu
 ting to emerging practices in our field.  Sponsored by the NYU M.A. in Hi
 storical and Sustainable Architecture  If you do not hold a current NYU I
 D\, please register here for building access:https://forms.gle/Ndwm2MTcf5
 dzzEzt9 (https://forms.gle/Ndwm2MTcf5dzzEzt9)  &#160\;
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  The presentation will explore the many\
 , often conflicting heritage and sustainability challenges in bringing a 
 derelict mediaeval chapel back into use as a contemporary home in rural E
 ngland. British architect Malcolm Fryer will outline his work\, focusing 
 on the ongoing project to repair and improve the St. Andrew's Chapel at B
 oxley in Kent. He will share details about his work with Historic England
  on thermal\, moisture and airtightness performance as a case study that 
 suggests directions and practices for the integration of efficiency and m
 odernization into historic structures. Professor Fryer will also share hi
 s experience in taking NYU MA students to visit the project over the last
  few years\, highlighting opportunities to participate in projects\, inve
 stigating and contributing to emerging practices in our field.</p><p styl
 e="font-weight: 400\;">  Sponsored by the NYU M.A. in Historical and Sust
 ainable Architecture</p><p style="font-weight: 400\;">  If you do not hol
 d a current NYU ID\, please register here for building access:</p><p styl
 e="font-weight: 400\;"><a href="https://forms.gle/Ndwm2MTcf5dzzEzt9">http
 s://forms.gle/Ndwm2MTcf5dzzEzt9</a></p><p style="font-weight: 400\;">  &#
 160\;</p>
