BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261022
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261023
LOCATION:N101\, ICA at NYU Shanghai
SUMMARY:Vitalisms II
DESCRIPTION:Vitalisms II is an exhibition-as-experiment that continues a 
 long-term research and education project on contemporary painting in Chin
 a. Following the first chapter's inquiry into the historicity of painting
 \, we now turn toward a different question: How does valuecome into being
  through our encounters with painting?  As the opening season of our new 
 artistic research program Untimely Inheritances (2026–28)\, Vitalisms I
 I considers inheritance as a condition we inhabit and reinterpret. Within
  the financial imagination\, value is rendered calculable\, comparable\, 
 and exchangeable. Yet value can also name artistic\, historical\, social\
 , institutional\, and affective relations. What happens when a painting i
 s encountered as an artwork\, cultural artifact\, collectible object\, co
 mmodity\, or financial asset?Vitalisms II focuses on abstract and non-rep
 resentational painting\, a field whose boundaries remain uncertain. The e
 xhibition brings together practices ranging from conceptually driven\, ha
 rd-edged geometric painting to more intuitive and expressive approaches\,
  including works that approach the threshold of representation. How might
  abstraction reveal\, rather than obscure\, relations that are otherwise 
 difficult to perceive? How might we keep interpretation open\, rather tha
 n seeking settlement? And what might speculation come to mean as a practi
 ce of imagining otherwise\, rather than predicting return?  The title Vit
 alisms draws from Isabelle Graw's account of painting's "vitalistic fanta
 sies"—how vitality and agency have been projected onto painting. *Vital
 isms I asked whether we could extend these fantasies and think of paintin
 gs as active participants in the historical and social relations through 
 which they are made\, encountered\, and circulated. Vitalisms II asks: Wh
 at might happen when we approach a painting not simply as an image to be 
 interpreted\, but as something with which we enter into relation?  Like t
 he first&#160\;Vitalisms\, this chapter is conceived as an experiment in 
 which knowledge is exchanged and produced throughout the exhibition. Duri
 ng weekly study sessions\, we spend extended time with the paintings\, de
 veloping interpretations collectively through close looking\, discussion\
 , and reflection. These encounters become part of the exhibition's resear
 ch\, inviting us to ask: What might we come to value—and\, in turn\, ma
 ke possible?  *&#160\;Isabelle Graw\, The Love of Painting (Sternberg Pre
 ss\, 2018): 21-24.——————————————————�
 ��——————————————————–Vitalisms II i
 s accompanied by related events\, including:  OPENING RECEPTIONFriday\, 1
 8 September\, 16:00–19:00        A public reception to celebrate the ex
 hibition's opening with food and drinks.STUDY SESSION (https://ica.shangh
 ai.nyu.edu/program/form-and-content-ii)  Form &amp\; Content II: How Do W
 e Value Painting(s)?Fridays\, 13:00–14:00 (English) / 16:00–17:00 PM 
 (Chinese)      9\, 16\, 23\, 30 October      6\, 13\, 20 November        
 For the artwork featured in each session and the full schedule\, please v
 isit the event webpage.  A weekly series of Study Sessions facilitated by
  ICA curators using Visual Thinking Strategies (VTS). Each one-hour sessi
 on focuses on a single painting\, inviting participants to spend time loo
 king and sharing their observations and interpretations through conversat
 ion. The sessions bring the public into Vitalisms II's research into how 
 value comes into being through our encounters with painting. No prior kno
 wledge of art or the exhibition is required.ROUNDTABLE (https://ica.shang
 hai.nyu.edu/program/after-looking)  After Looking: What Does Looking Have
  to Do with Value?Saturday\, 5 December\, 13:30-17:00        A roundtable
  bringing together the artists exhibiting in&#160\;Vitalisms II and the I
 CA curators who facilitated the exhibition's Study Sessions. Over seven w
 eeks\, the public spent sustained time with individual paintings through 
 close looking and group discussion. From these sessions\, the curators wi
 ll bring selected observations\, interpretations\, and questions into con
 versation with the artists\, exploring how paintings acquire meaning and 
 value through different encounters.  All events are FREE and OPEN to the 
 public.  For further details and to register for events\, please visit th
 e event pages under Related programs below.  —————————�
 ��———————————————————————�
 �———–  The exhibition Vitalisms II and related events are present
 ed as the first season of the ICA's artistic research program Untimely In
 heritances (2026–28)\, attending to what inheritance asks of us and how
  we might inhabit unfinished worlds through practices of interpretation\,
  response\, and transformation.Vitalisms II is organized by Michelle Yeon
 ho Hyun with BAO Yang and CHEN Baichao\, with additional support by HOU C
 huchu\, QU Yanqiu and JIAO Meiqi. Special thanks to Arts &amp\; Collectio
 ns Foundation\, Luoying Sheng\, Han Xiao\, and Chong Zhou for their gener
 osity\, as well as Antenna Space\, CLC Gallery\, Magician Space\, Ota Fin
 e Arts\, ShanghART Gallery and ZIAN Gallery\, for their support. Graphic 
 Design by LIU Anqi. Exhibition Design by FANG Yuan.  Our deepest gratitud
 e belongs to the artists for sharing their knowledge\, work\, and time wi
 th us\, extending our curiosity and creating more questions.  Early resea
 rch and development for&#160\;Untimely Inheritances received kind support
  from Goethe-Institut\, as well as research assistance from Oviya Dinakha
 r\, Fay Guan\, and Lizzy Xie.&#160\;
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p dir="ltr"><em>Vitalisms II</em> is an exh
 ibition-as-experiment that continues a long-term research and education p
 roject on contemporary painting in China. Following the first chapter's i
 nquiry into the historicity of painting\, we now turn toward a different 
 question: <strong>How does <em>value</em></strong><strong>come into being
  through our encounters with painting?</strong><br /><br />  As the openi
 ng season of our new artistic research program <em>Untimely Inheritances<
 /em> (2026–28)\, <em>Vitalisms II</em> considers inheritance as a condi
 tion we inhabit and reinterpret. Within the financial imagination\, value
  is rendered calculable\, comparable\, and exchangeable. Yet value can al
 so name artistic\, historical\, social\, institutional\, and affective re
 lations. What happens when a painting is encountered as an artwork\, cult
 ural artifact\, collectible object\, commodity\, or financial asset?<br /
 ><br /><em>Vitalisms II</em> focuses on abstract and non-representational
  painting\, a field whose boundaries remain uncertain. The exhibition bri
 ngs together practices ranging from conceptually driven\, hard-edged geom
 etric painting to more intuitive and expressive approaches\, including wo
 rks that approach the threshold of representation. How might abstraction 
 reveal\, rather than obscure\, relations that are otherwise difficult to 
 perceive? How might we keep interpretation open\, rather than seeking set
 tlement? And what might speculation come to mean as a practice of imagini
 ng otherwise\, rather than predicting return?<br /><br />  The title <em>
 Vitalisms</em> draws from Isabelle Graw's account of painting's "vitalist
 ic fantasies"—how vitality and agency have been projected onto painting
 . *<em>Vitalisms I</em> asked whether we could extend these fantasies and
  think of paintings as active participants in the historical and social r
 elations through which they are made\, encountered\, and circulated. <em>
 Vitalisms II</em> asks: What might happen when we approach a painting not
  simply as an image to be interpreted\, but as something with which we en
 ter into relation?<br /><br />  Like the first&#160\;<em>Vitalisms</em>\,
  this chapter is conceived as an experiment in which knowledge is exchang
 ed and produced throughout the exhibition. During weekly study sessions\,
  we spend extended time with the paintings\, developing interpretations c
 ollectively through close looking\, discussion\, and reflection. These en
 counters become part of the exhibition's research\, inviting us to ask: <
 strong>What might we come to value—and\, in turn\, make possible?</stro
 ng></p><p dir="ltr">  *&#160\;Isabelle Graw\, <em>The Love of Painting</e
 m> (Sternberg Press\, 2018): 21-24.<strong><br /></strong>—————
 ————————————————————————�
 ��———————–<br /></p><p><em>Vitalisms II</em> is accompani
 ed by related events\, including:<br /><br />  OPENING RECEPTION<br /></p
 ><ul><li><ul><li>Friday\, 18 September\, 16:00–19:00      </li></ul></l
 i></ul><p>  A public reception to celebrate the exhibition's opening with
  food and drinks.<br /></p><p><a href="https://ica.shanghai.nyu.edu/progr
 am/form-and-content-ii" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">STUDY S
 ESSION</a><br />  Form &amp\; Content II: How Do We Value Painting(s)?<br
  /></p><ul><li><ul><li>Fridays\, 13:00–14:00 (English) / 16:00–17:00 
 PM (Chinese)      </li><li>9\, 16\, 23\, 30 October      </li><li>6\, 13\
 , 20 November      </li></ul></li></ul><p>  For the artwork featured in e
 ach session and the full schedule\, please visit the event webpage.<br />
 </p><p>  A weekly series of Study Sessions facilitated by ICA curators us
 ing Visual Thinking Strategies (VTS). Each one-hour session focuses on a 
 single painting\, inviting participants to spend time looking and sharing
  their observations and interpretations through conversation. The session
 s bring the public into <em>Vitalisms II's</em> research into how value c
 omes into being through our encounters with painting. No prior knowledge 
 of art or the exhibition is required.<br /></p><p dir="ltr"><a href="http
 s://ica.shanghai.nyu.edu/program/after-looking" target="_blank" rel="noop
 ener noreferrer">ROUNDTABLE</a><br />  After Looking: What Does Looking H
 ave to Do with Value?<br /></p><ul><li><ul><li dir="ltr">Saturday\, 5 Dec
 ember\, 13:30-17:00      </li></ul></li></ul><p dir="ltr">  A roundtable 
 bringing together the artists exhibiting in&#160\;<em>Vitalisms II</em> a
 nd the ICA curators who facilitated the exhibition's Study Sessions. Over
  seven weeks\, the public spent sustained time with individual paintings 
 through close looking and group discussion. From these sessions\, the cur
 ators will bring selected observations\, interpretations\, and questions 
 into conversation with the artists\, exploring how paintings acquire mean
 ing and value through different encounters.<br /></p><p dir="ltr"><br /> 
  All events are FREE and OPEN to the public.<br />  For further details a
 nd to register for events\, please visit the event pages under Related pr
 ograms below.<br /></p><div>  ——————————————�
 ��——————————————————————–<b
 r /></div><p dir="ltr">  The exhibition <em>Vitalisms II</em> and related
  events are presented as the first season of the ICA's artistic research 
 program <em>Untimely Inheritances</em> (2026–28)\, attending to what in
 heritance asks of us and how we might inhabit unfinished worlds through p
 ractices of interpretation\, response\, and transformation.<br /><br /><e
 m>Vitalisms II</em> is organized by Michelle Yeonho Hyun with BAO Yang an
 d CHEN Baichao\, with additional support by HOU Chuchu\, QU Yanqiu and JI
 AO Meiqi. Special thanks to Arts &amp\; Collections Foundation\, Luoying 
 Sheng\, Han Xiao\, and Chong Zhou for their generosity\, as well as Anten
 na Space\, CLC Gallery\, Magician Space\, Ota Fine Arts\, ShanghART Galle
 ry and ZIAN Gallery\, for their support. Graphic Design by LIU Anqi. Exhi
 bition Design by FANG Yuan.<br /><br />  Our deepest gratitude belongs to
  the artists for sharing their knowledge\, work\, and time with us\, exte
 nding our curiosity and creating more questions.<br /><br />  Early resea
 rch and development for&#160\;<em>Untimely Inheritances</em> received kin
 d support from Goethe-Institut\, as well as research assistance from Oviy
 a Dinakhar\, Fay Guan\, and Lizzy Xie.&#160\;<br /></p>
