LOCATION:7 East 12th Street\, New York\, New York\, 10003
GEO:40.734578;-73.993586
SUMMARY:Navigating Global Challenges—Rethinking Readiness with Jeff Sch
 legelmilch
DESCRIPTION:As the nature of disasters becomes increasingly complex—fro
 m climate-driven hazards and pandemics to cascading infrastructure failur
 es\, geopolitical instability\, and systemic disruption—traditional app
 roaches to preparedness are being challenged. Mr. Schegelmilch will offer
  a forward-looking perspective on what it means to be truly ready in an e
 ra of interconnected and compounding risks.  This thought-provoking conve
 rsation will explore how governments\, businesses\,  communities\, and in
 dividuals can rethink readiness—not simply as the ability to respond to
  a known hazard\, but as the capacity to anticipate uncertainty\, adapt t
 o disruption\, and build resilience before crisis strikes.  In this sessi
 on\, Chloe Demrovsky\, Executive in Residence and Clinical Visiting  Assi
 stant Professor in Global Business and Economy at NYU SPS Center for Glob
 al Affairs\, will engage Mr. Schegelmilch in a dynamic conversation drawi
 ng on his extensive experience in disaster preparedness\, resilience\, em
 ergency management\, and risk reduction. Together\, they will examine wha
 t the next generation of readiness should look like—and how we can move
  from preparing for individual disasters to building systems capable of n
 avigating a world of cascading and interconnected risks.  This roundtable
  is part of a series of intimate discussions with influential figures sha
 ping the future of the global economy.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  As the nature of disasters becomes incr
 easingly complex—from climate-driven hazards and pandemics to cascading
  infrastructure failures\, geopolitical instability\, and systemic disrup
 tion—traditional approaches to preparedness are being challenged. Mr. S
 chegelmilch will offer a forward-looking perspective on what it means to 
 be truly ready in an era of interconnected and compounding risks.</p><p> 
  This thought-provoking conversation will explore how governments\, busin
 esses\,<br />  communities\, and individuals can rethink readiness—not 
 simply as the ability to respond to a known hazard\, but as the capacity 
 to anticipate uncertainty\, adapt to disruption\, and build resilience be
 fore crisis strikes.</p><p>  In this session\, Chloe Demrovsky\, Executiv
 e in Residence and Clinical Visiting<br />  Assistant Professor in Global
  Business and Economy at NYU SPS Center for Global Affairs\, will engage 
 Mr. Schegelmilch in a dynamic conversation drawing on his extensive exper
 ience in disaster preparedness\, resilience\, emergency management\, and 
 risk reduction. Together\, they will examine what the next generation of 
 readiness should look like—and how we can move from preparing for indiv
 idual disasters to building systems capable of navigating a world of casc
 ading and interconnected risks.</p><p>  This roundtable is part of a seri
 es of intimate discussions with influential figures shaping the future of
  the global economy.</p>
UID:20261022T210000Z-403820@events.nyu.edu
