SUMMARY:An Evening with Anne Fadiman: Looking back on The Spirit Catches 
 You and You Fall Down after three decades
DESCRIPTION:Nearly thirty years ago\, Fadiman's sensitive story of Lia Le
 e\, a Hmong child with epilepsy\, and her American doctors\, brought the 
 complexities of cross-cultural medicine to a wide audience and changed th
 e way that medicine is practiced and understood in the United States.  Th
 e book continues to be taught at universities both as literary journalism
  and as a casebook for cross-cultural practice in general\; it is also wi
 dely read by medical students and practitioners who wish to offer more ef
 fective care to patients from other cultures.  As The New York Times' obi
 tuary of Lia Lee noted\, "Lia's story\, as few other narratives have done
 \, has had a significant effect on the ways in which American medicine is
  practiced across cultures\, and on the training of doctors."  On October
  22\, Fadiman will speak with NYU Professor Perri Klass about how the boo
 k came to be\, and how its lessons about medicine\, culture\, and care st
 ill resonate today.  &#160\;REGISTER HERE (https://www.eventbrite.com/e/2
 001217809339?aff=oddtdtcreator)
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p><picture class="lw_image lw_image146158 l
 w_align_left lw_column_width_half"><source type="image/webp" srcset="http
 s://events.nyu.edu/live/image/scale/2x/gid/138/width/200/height/300/14615
 8_Anne_Fadiman.webp 2x\, https://events.nyu.edu/live/image/scale/3x/gid/1
 38/width/200/height/300/146158_Anne_Fadiman.webp 3x" data-origin="respons
 ive"/><source type="image/jpeg" srcset="https://events.nyu.edu/live/image
 /scale/2x/gid/138/width/200/height/300/146158_Anne_Fadiman.rev.1791213686
 .jpg 2x\, https://events.nyu.edu/live/image/scale/3x/gid/138/width/200/he
 ight/300/146158_Anne_Fadiman.rev.1791213686.jpg 3x" data-origin="responsi
 ve"/><img width="200" height="300" alt="Cover of book: The Spirit Catches
  You and You Fall Down." data-alt="Cover of book: The Spirit Catches You 
 and You Fall Down." src="https://events.nyu.edu/live/image/gid/138/width/
 200/height/300/146158_Anne_Fadiman.rev.1791213686.jpg" title="The Spirit 
 Catches You and You Fall Down" srcset="https://events.nyu.edu/live/image/
 scale/2x/gid/138/width/200/height/300/146158_Anne_Fadiman.rev.1791213686.
 jpg 2x\, https://events.nyu.edu/live/image/scale/3x/gid/138/width/200/hei
 ght/300/146158_Anne_Fadiman.rev.1791213686.jpg 3x" data-max-w="900" data-
 max-h="1352" loading="lazy" data-optimized="true"/></picture>Nearly thirt
 y years ago\, Fadiman's sensitive story of Lia Lee\, a Hmong child with e
 pilepsy\, and her American doctors\, brought the complexities of cross-cu
 ltural medicine to a wide audience and changed the way that medicine is p
 racticed and understood in the United States.</p><p>  The book continues 
 to be taught at universities both as literary journalism and as a caseboo
 k for cross-cultural practice in general\; it is also widely read by medi
 cal students and practitioners who wish to offer more effective care to p
 atients from other cultures.</p><p>  As <i>The New York Times</i>' obitua
 ry of Lia Lee noted\, "Lia's story\, as few other narratives have done\, 
 has had a significant effect on the ways in which American medicine is pr
 acticed across cultures\, and on the training of doctors."</p><p>  On Oct
 ober 22\, Fadiman will speak with NYU Professor Perri Klass about how the
  book came to be\, and how its lessons about medicine\, culture\, and car
 e still resonate today.</p><p>  &#160\;</p><p><strong><a href="https://ww
 w.eventbrite.com/e/2001217809339?aff=oddtdtcreator">REGISTER HERE</a></st
 rong></p>
