LOCATION:Main Event Space\, Rm. 101\, 105 East 17th Street
GEO:40.736399;-73.988013
SUMMARY:ENVIRONMENTAL CRISIS IN SOUTHEAST ASIA: POLITICS\, MISRULE\, AND 
 RESISTANCE
DESCRIPTION:This conference brings together scholars and practitioners to
  examine the politics of environmental crisis across Southeast Asia—a r
 egion where socio-ecological ruptures are not aberrations but products of
  longstanding structures of extraction and governance failure.Panelists t
 race the roots of these crises and assess how extractive industries\, inc
 reasingly legitimized through the language of green transitions and clima
 te finance\, reproduce entrenched patterns of dispossession and injustice
 . From nickel mining and biofuel expansion to sand dredging and flood aid
  corruption\, the panels situate contemporary crises within deeper histor
 ies of resource conflict and structural inequality.The conference foregro
 unds questions of resistance and accountability: what are communities\, a
 ctivists\, and civil society organizations doing to contest new forms of 
 ecological crisis? And what political conditions are necessary to advance
  climate action while achieving social and environmental justice? Beyond 
 critical analysis\, we seek insights into the strategies—legal\, politi
 cal\, and grassroots—that have worked or might work to address Southeas
 t Asia's intertwined ecological and democratic crises.RSVP: https://wagne
 r.nyu.edu/community/events/environmental-crisis-southeast-asia-politics-m
 isrule-and-resistance-2026-10-23 (https://wagner.nyu.edu/community/events
 /environmental-crisis-southeast-asia-politics-misrule-and-resistance-2026
 -10-23)
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:This conference brings together scholars and
  practitioners to examine the politics of environmental crisis across Sou
 theast Asia—a region where socio-ecological ruptures are not aberration
 s but products of longstanding structures of extraction and governance fa
 ilure.<br />Panelists trace the roots of these crises and assess how extr
 active industries\, increasingly legitimized through the language of gree
 n transitions and climate finance\, reproduce entrenched patterns of disp
 ossession and injustice. From nickel mining and biofuel expansion to sand
  dredging and flood aid corruption\, the panels situate contemporary cris
 es within deeper histories of resource conflict and structural inequality
 .<br />The conference foregrounds questions of resistance and accountabil
 ity: what are communities\, activists\, and civil society organizations d
 oing to contest new forms of ecological crisis? And what political condit
 ions are necessary to advance climate action while achieving social and e
 nvironmental justice? Beyond critical analysis\, we seek insights into th
 e strategies—legal\, political\, and grassroots—that have worked or m
 ight work to address Southeast Asia's intertwined ecological and democrat
 ic crises.<br />RSVP: <a href="https://wagner.nyu.edu/community/events/en
 vironmental-crisis-southeast-asia-politics-misrule-and-resistance-2026-10
 -23" target="_blank">https://wagner.nyu.edu/community/events/environmenta
 l-crisis-southeast-asia-politics-misrule-and-resistance-2026-10-23</a>
