DTSTART;TZID=America/New_York:20261023T160000
DTEND;TZID=America/New_York:20261023T173000
LOCATION:5 Washington Place\, Room 202\, New York\, NY 10003
SUMMARY:Bioethics Colloquium: On Chaos\, What we Ought to Do\, and What w
 e Ought to Regret
DESCRIPTION:Hosted by the Center for BioethicsJoin the NYU Center for Bio
 ethics for a colloquium with Caspar Hare\, Professor of Philosophy in the
  Department of Linguistics and Philosophy at MIT\, whose work spans ethic
 s\, practical rationality\, and metaphysics. Drawing on his new book\, Li
 ving in a Strange World\, Professor Hare will explore a striking idea: fo
 r almost every action ever taken\, future generations can correctly say t
 hat if it had not happened\, they almost certainly would not exist. He ar
 gues that this has no surprising implications for what we ought to do\, b
 ut very surprising implications for what we ought to regret. Attendees wi
 ll see how big ideas from cosmology\, physics\, and metaphysics can bear 
 on everyday moral life\, and how they might change the way we think about
  past wrongs and our own existence. The talk will be followed by a recept
 ion.https://publichealth.nyu.edu/events-news/events/2026/10/23/bioethics-
 colloquium-chaos-what-we-ought-do-and-what-we-ought-regret (https://publi
 chealth.nyu.edu/events-news/events/2026/10/23/bioethics-colloquium-chaos-
 what-we-ought-do-and-what-we-ought-regret)
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:Hosted by the Center for BioethicsJoin the N
 YU Center for Bioethics for a colloquium with Caspar Hare\, Professor of 
 Philosophy in the Department of Linguistics and Philosophy at MIT\, whose
  work spans ethics\, practical rationality\, and metaphysics. Drawing on 
 his new book\, Living in a Strange World\, Professor Hare will explore a 
 striking idea: for almost every action ever taken\, future generations ca
 n correctly say that if it had not happened\, they almost certainly would
  not exist. He argues that this has no surprising implications for what w
 e ought to do\, but very surprising implications for what we ought to reg
 ret. Attendees will see how big ideas from cosmology\, physics\, and meta
 physics can bear on everyday moral life\, and how they might change the w
 ay we think about past wrongs and our own existence. The talk will be fol
 lowed by a reception.<br /><a href="https://publichealth.nyu.edu/events-n
 ews/events/2026/10/23/bioethics-colloquium-chaos-what-we-ought-do-and-wha
 t-we-ought-regret" target="_blank">https://publichealth.nyu.edu/events-ne
 ws/events/2026/10/23/bioethics-colloquium-chaos-what-we-ought-do-and-what
 -we-ought-regret</a>
